#data

// comment
@variable = 0xFFFF

// constants can either be numbers or ascii strings
// instruction shorthands(such as LDI) are actually just predefined constants because i'm really nice to users

#text

// start function
// in the second set of square brackets, the first number denotes a core and the second number denotes where the function pointer belongs
// the idea behind this is that you can easily declare interrupt handlers by declaring what core its for and how it works(where empty is just the start function)
// the third set of square brackets is for arguments which will be accessible as constants

[start] [0] ()
@LDI $0 @variable

// dollar sign denotes a register.
// constants are either written as numbers or strings

@HLT
[/start]

// the line above signals the end of the function

// The really cool part about this is that the asterisk denotes that the same function be used for all cores.
// An interrupt in your system that uses a common handler for all cores can easily be set up as such! 
[mutex_permission_failure] [* 2] ()
@HLT
[/mutex_permission_failure]
